00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.150
them so powerful for very
specific applications.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.410
And so this article--

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.520
it's an explanation
of bfloat16, which

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.680
is kind of the secret
of the high performance

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.050
that you see with Cloud TPUs.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.720
So it goes into some
of the math behind it,

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.175
and how TPUs take
advantage of that.

00:04:16.175 --> 00:04:17.550
And what I like
about the article

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.910
is that the bfloat16 approach
uses mixed precision,

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:24.030
and that's suitable for
certain types of machine

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.950
learning algorithms,
and it's not very well

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.340
suited to other ones.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.460
So this article
details the performance

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.190
that they saw using
different approaches.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.670
And I also came across a
paper that some researchers

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.380
