00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:00.670
CHRIS ALBON: Sure.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.800
I am the Director
of Data Science

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.400
for Devoted Health, which is
a Medicare Advantage startup.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.102
So it is a health
insurance company.

00:10:08.102 --> 00:10:09.310
That's what everyone asks me.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:11.140
Everyone's like oh, yeah,
you're like a health startup.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.182
Like you should probably
use AI to connect people

00:10:13.182 --> 00:10:14.432
to health insurance companies.

00:10:14.432 --> 00:10:15.160
Like, no, no, no.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:17.690
This is like a regular
health insurance company

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:18.920
that we started from scratch.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:20.758
So it's like, you
know, like actual cards

00:10:20.758 --> 00:10:23.050
from the health insurance
company like, the full setup.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.550
And it's a really interesting
experience from a data science

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:29.170
perspective, because you come
in with very little knowledge

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:31.278
of how health insurance
actually works in America.

00:10:31.278 --> 00:10:33.070
And then you sit down
and then you actually

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:35.190
talk to a bunch of experts
who work [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.250
and you go, oh, wow,
this is complicated.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:38.500
This is super-complicated.

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:39.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I
don't think anyone's

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.500
dealt with American
insurance and been

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:42.640
like, no, that was easy.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:43.310
[LAUGHING]

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:43.810
No.

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:45.178
This is all straightforward.

00:10:45.178 --> 00:10:46.720
You'll figure it
out in a day or two.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:47.230
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:48.230
Well that was the thing.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:50.237
When I joined, or
before I joined,

00:10:50.237 --> 00:10:52.570
I did a bunch of reading of
these like, general audience

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:53.362
books on insurance.

00:10:53.362 --> 00:10:55.150
And I was thinking
I was pretty smart.

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:58.180
And then DJ Patil, my
boss, was like, how much

00:10:58.180 --> 00:10:59.590
you think you know.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:00.490
You know nothing.

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:05.163
And now I realize, like, oh,
oh, this is crazy complicated.

00:11:05.163 --> 00:11:06.580
MICHELLE CASBON:
So are you saying

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:08.122
that that was actually
a harder thing

00:11:08.122 --> 00:11:09.893
to learn than the
data science side?

00:11:09.893 --> 00:11:10.810
CHRIS ALBON: Oh, yeah.

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:13.750
No, I mean, I actually think
the specialty on our team

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:17.050
is taking folks who are very,
very strong in data science,

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:18.852
and then teaching
them about Medicare,

00:11:18.852 --> 00:11:20.560
and teaching them
about health insurance.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.240
Because there's a whole other
realm of information in there.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:25.960
And it's something that
we take very seriously.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:27.770
Because if you do
the analysis wrong,

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.853
like you can actually
affect people's care, right,

00:11:29.853 --> 00:11:30.620
in a real way.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.240
This is how people get
their health insurance.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:33.740
This is how they
pay for the doctor.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:35.930
This is how they get
their prescription drugs.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:38.530
And if you do something wrong,
we have to know immediately,

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:39.950
and we have to fix it.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:41.540
And so bringing
people on and saying,

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:43.200
hey, let's teach
you about Medicare,

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.700
let's teach you about Medicare
Advantage, that kind of stuff

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:47.560
is actually a lot of what we do.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:49.530
We have a lot of
educational sessions.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:50.530
We have a lot of, like--

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:52.243
we actually have a
channel in our Slack.

00:11:52.243 --> 00:11:54.160
So we use Slack for our
company communication.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:55.660
We actually have a
channel where people are

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:56.827
allowed to ask any question.

00:11:56.827 --> 00:11:58.900
You can ask any, any, any
question that you want.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:00.560
You can ask as many
times as you want.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:01.990
You can ask for clarification.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.880
And the idea behind
the whole thing

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:05.890
is to say, no, don't
assume that you know it.

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:06.730
Just ask someone.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:07.640
Just ask someone.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:09.970
And, you know, that's how
we'll work this thing out.

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:12.430
Because there have
been cases of startups

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.280
who come into the
health insurance base,

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:15.800
that they made some assumption.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:17.925
And that assumption ended
up hurting people's care,

00:12:17.925 --> 00:12:20.007
because they decided that
a high number was good,

00:12:20.007 --> 00:12:21.340
and in fact a high number's bad.

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:21.580
Right.

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:23.710
And then that, like,
effects people's ability

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:24.950
to go to the doctor.

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:26.580
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it's
kind of like three-parted

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.030
in the approach that
people need to take.

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:28.210
Right?

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.130
Because there's this classic
data science background,

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:31.963
full of best practices
and tips, and there's

00:12:31.963 --> 00:12:34.770
kind of a general
understanding of what that is.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:36.520
But you can't really
work in this industry

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:38.312
without also knowing
the second part, which

00:12:38.312 --> 00:12:40.540
is going to be all the
health care specific data,

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.650
the nuances, the people
you're working with.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:43.650
And then the third
part I'm guessing

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.700
is going to be a lot of
regulation around that.

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:47.758
So, I mean, like, you
know, if you're coming in

00:12:47.758 --> 00:12:49.675
and you've been a data
scientist for 20 years,

00:12:49.675 --> 00:12:52.567
you know a lot of this stuff,
you're not fully prepared

00:12:52.567 --> 00:12:54.400
to take this on without
additional training,

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:55.540
additional understanding.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.770
You can't just dive in.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:57.520
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:12:59.110
No, it's this
amazing thing where

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:02.193
obviously Medicare is for
people who are 65 and older.

00:13:02.193 --> 00:13:04.610
We basically have no one on
staff who's 65 years or older.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:06.220
So we can't dog
food our own thing.

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:07.660
Like my health insurance
from the company

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:09.660
is not my own health
insurance, despite the fact

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.600
we are a health
insurance company.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.450
And one of the most
important things that we do,

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:15.970
which was actually
started by DJ,

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:18.770
was that we have data science
folks actually go to the field.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:20.620
So for example, like,
I flew down to Florida

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:22.305
and actually visited
doctor's offices.

00:13:22.305 --> 00:13:23.680
Actually we have
our own doctors,

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:25.510
so I actually went
on a patient visit

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:28.480
and sat in one of our member's
houses while one of our doctors

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:30.327
actually did after
hospitalization.

00:13:30.327 --> 00:13:31.660
And the idea is to make it real.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:32.160
Right.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:33.640
Like this isn't
just a data point.

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:34.840
This isn't something
where you think

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:35.923
what you're talking about.

00:13:35.923 --> 00:13:38.020
Like, go and sit down
and see it on the ground,

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:41.760
and see the reality, and then go
back and work from that point.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:43.600
Like, do the analyses
from that point.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.340
Build the models
from that point.

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:48.120
And if, in health insurance,
if you don't have that,

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:49.850
I don't think you
could do the job.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:51.720
I don't know how it would work.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.553
MICHELLE CASBON: So
what was your motivation

00:13:53.553 --> 00:13:54.390
for joining Devoted?

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:56.140
I know you may be new
to health insurance,

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:57.557
but you're not new
to health care.

00:13:57.557 --> 00:13:58.130
Right?

00:13:58.130 --> 00:13:58.797
CHRIS ALBON: No.

00:13:58.797 --> 00:14:01.690
So my background is
in humanitarian tech.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:04.500
So I worked with a number
of Kenyan nonprofits

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.900
like Ushahidi and
FrontlineSMS, and did things

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:09.600
like election monitoring
and global health projects.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.610
And after that I
spun out a company

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:14.100
with a few of my friends who
I used to work with Ushahidi,

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:16.710
that now works on fake news
stuff, called New Knowledge.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:19.110
And then I went back
after my startup

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.090
and went back and worked for
a Kenyan social good program.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:25.530
And so my whole background is
in doing some things that have

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:26.820
some kind of social impact.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.737
That's the thing that I
have had the privilege

00:14:28.737 --> 00:14:30.370
of doing for a very long time.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:33.058
And there was just this
one day where DJ, you know,

00:14:33.058 --> 00:14:33.600
called me up.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.130
And he was like,
hey, I really need

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:36.540
you to help me fix Medicare.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:37.920
And I was like [BLEEP].

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:38.820
[LAUGHING]

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:39.350
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh yeah.

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:39.900
CHRIS ALBON: Can I
swear on this podcast?

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:40.350
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:41.115
[LAUGHING]

00:14:41.115 --> 00:14:42.870
CHRIS ALBON: I think that
was basically my reaction.

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:43.320
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Of course.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:44.497
Yeah, I mean, that's no--

00:14:44.497 --> 00:14:46.830
as we just talked about,
that's not an easy thing to do.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:48.660
I mean, there's plenty of
issues with it currently,

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.180
and there's a lot
of concerns there.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.510
But it's also an
incredibly complex field.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:53.260
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:55.135
And that was my big
thing coming in was like,

00:14:55.135 --> 00:14:56.940
I'm not trying to
improve everything.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:57.960
I'm not trying to
fix everything.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:14:58.950
I'm not trying to do--

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:00.180
but if I could take
one step and make

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:01.430
it a little bit better, right.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:04.772
Like if we could build something
that we would want, as Devoted

00:15:04.772 --> 00:15:07.230
says in their slogan, like,
that you'd want your own family

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:08.430
members to be on,
then you're good.

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:08.690
Right.

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:09.150
Like that's it.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:11.050
Just one step forward
that's a little bit better,

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:12.810
that's a little bit nicer,
that has a little bit more,

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:14.640
you know, like, a
health insurance company

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:15.870
with a bit of a soul.

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:16.410
Great.

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:17.670
We've pushed the bar forward.

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.210
People's lives are better.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:21.660
The grand sweeping
change is far beyond my--

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:22.830
[LAUGHING]

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:24.128
--my ability to fix.

00:15:24.128 --> 00:15:26.670
MICHELLE CASBON: So you're not
taking your global health care

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:29.415
perspective and applying that
to the American health insurance

00:15:29.415 --> 00:15:29.580
industry?

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:30.330
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:31.080
No, no.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.210
I mean, I think the
thing that I take

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:36.945
from it is a really, really,
really strong humility

00:15:36.945 --> 00:15:38.070
towards my own assumptions.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:38.220
Right.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.595
When you work at
a Kenyan company

00:15:39.595 --> 00:15:42.300
or what I was doing, like
flying down to Mozambique

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:44.627
and doing some project, you
have to trust the people

00:15:44.627 --> 00:15:45.210
on the ground.

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:47.140
They know what it's like to
work in those environments.

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:48.660
They know what it's
like, like how far they

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.730
can push the government before
the government retaliates.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:52.180
Like, they know all that stuff.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:53.220
And so you have to
come in as someone

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:55.380
who knows a lot about data
and can manipulate data

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:58.855
and can work with them, but
basing that on their knowledge.

00:15:58.855 --> 00:16:01.230
And that's what I've really
tried to instill with my team

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.570
at Devoted is, hey, we
have all these people

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.250
who've been working in this
industry for a long time.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.290
We have a bunch of providers
in the field who know

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:08.190
what it's like to be in there.

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:09.780
Like, trust the
person in the field.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.010
Trust their knowledge.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.560
Help them and support them,
but don't walk in and say,

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:14.730
there's some logistic
regression that's

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:15.580
going to solve all of this.

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:17.460
Let me just figure
out which one it is.

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:18.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I think
that's like the ultimate form

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:21.870
of advocacy is that true-- not
only that level of empathy,

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.450
but also that level of just
connecting with your audience

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.720
and working with them to say,
look, you know your area,

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:28.800
I know my area.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:29.643
Let's combine that.

00:16:29.643 --> 00:16:30.810
Let's make something happen.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:31.560
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:33.400
The really fun thing
about data science

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:36.468
is, you have the ability to
work in a whole bunch of areas

00:16:36.468 --> 00:16:37.260
and work with data.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:39.407
And you can, in a bad way,
come to the assumption

00:16:39.407 --> 00:16:41.490
that you could fix everything,
that you understand

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:42.817
how the data is generated.

00:16:42.817 --> 00:16:45.150
So you understand how the
data model works, and thus you

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:46.380
could figure out
every single thing,

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.030
and you can come up with
these grand theories,

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:49.390
and then write a model
and it fits them.

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:50.310
And then you say, hey, I'm done.

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:50.810
Right.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:51.397
We're good.

00:16:51.397 --> 00:16:52.980
And that's not true
in a lot of cases.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:53.480
Right.

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:55.440
Like, your assumptions
for how the world works

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.593
is not true, unless
you've gone and seen it,

00:16:57.593 --> 00:16:59.260
unless you've talked
to a lot of people,

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:01.750
unless you've sort of done
the legwork to figure it out.

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:04.026
And we try to do that a lot.

00:17:04.026 --> 00:17:06.359
I think one of the greatest
things of working at Devoted

00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:07.950
is the large number
of people that we

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.848
have who have worked in Medicare
Advantage for a long time,

00:17:10.848 --> 00:17:12.765
which means that I can
go to them and be like,

00:17:12.765 --> 00:17:14.910
I don't understand how
this thing happens.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:17.730
I'm constantly asking like,
the really stupid questions.

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:21.057
Because otherwise I'm terrified
of not getting it right.

00:17:21.057 --> 00:17:22.890
MICHELLE CASBON: During
your time at Devoted

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:26.730
you came in pretty early
on, and you built things up

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:27.510
from scratch.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:28.980
Tell us a little
bit about what it

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:31.522
was like to come in, not knowing
a whole lot about the space,

00:17:31.522 --> 00:17:33.510
and asking the right
questions, and making sure

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:34.552
that you stayed on track.

00:17:34.552 --> 00:17:37.170
What was that building up
a data science team like?

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.060
How did you go from
zero to something?

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:39.990
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:44.520
So when I came in, there was,
I think three data scientists,

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:45.447
all under DJ.

00:17:45.447 --> 00:17:47.280
And then DJ moved to
the head of technology,

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.420
and so then I took over
the data science team.

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:51.540
And we had to sit down,
me a DJ, and say, hey,

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.220
like, what's the kind of
team that we want to build?

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:53.340
Right.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:54.480
There's a variety
of ways that you

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.563
can implement data science
at any kind of company,

00:17:56.563 --> 00:17:57.510
from scratch.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.310
Do you have the data
scientist sort of sit

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:01.110
in the corner as a research lab?

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:04.250
Do you make the I-team just
takes tickets, and just

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.750
processes the tickets, and then
sends out the dashboard that

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:07.380
makes it work?

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:09.725
Those were both valid
in very common ways.

00:18:09.725 --> 00:18:11.850
We decided to go for the
fully integrated approach.

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:14.460
So every single data
scientist at Devoted

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.850
works in at least one other
team-- like actual teams that

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:20.550
are working on specific things,
like, say, processing claims,

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.237
or saying, talking
to our doctors.

00:18:22.237 --> 00:18:24.570
They sit in those meetings
and they are the data science

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.250
resource in those meetings.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:28.940
And that means that no one
has to submit a ticket when

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.940
they want to get something
done on data science.

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:32.370
They just tap the person
on the shoulder who's

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:33.953
sitting in their
meeting and say, hey,

00:18:33.953 --> 00:18:36.450
you know, like, Jason,
fix this for me.

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:37.503
That is super powerful.

00:18:37.503 --> 00:18:39.420
And I think that fits
our model of making sure

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:41.378
that the people who are
working in data science

00:18:41.378 --> 00:18:44.010
actually know the subject
matter that they're working on.

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.890
That kind of stuff, plus a
thousand other decisions, those

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:48.480
were the decisions that you
make when you start a team.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:49.710
Like, how do you
want to structure it?

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.000
How do you want to
build out this thing?

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:51.810
How many people do you want?

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:53.102
What's the seniority of people?

00:18:53.102 --> 00:18:55.200
And like walking
through that, slowly,

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.240
is my job all the time now.

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:59.490
MICHELLE CASBON: And did you
organize things that way?

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.715
So are people reporting
to the subject area teams?

00:19:01.715 --> 00:19:03.840
Or do you still have the
data scientists under you,

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.168
and then everyone just sort
of belongs to another team?

00:19:06.168 --> 00:19:07.960
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah, so
everyone is under me.

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:09.990
And then we lend them
out to the other teams.

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:12.000
And they'll jump
around a few times.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.300
But the goal is that if someone
is working on something that

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.850
needs data science, they
don't need to come to me

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:20.142
and say, hey, can I get
a resource for this?

00:19:20.142 --> 00:19:22.350
Or I submitted a Jira ticket
to make this thing work.

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:23.760
They can actually say,
no, no, no, you know,

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:24.570
they've been sitting
in our meeting

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:26.080
for weeks and weeks and weeks.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:27.955
They can totally understand
what's happening.

00:19:27.955 --> 00:19:29.850
They understand the
nuances of this.

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:31.050
Let's just have them do it.

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:32.290
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
based on your description,

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:34.650
I mean, it sounds like it's
a pretty successful model.

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:36.483
Like that's something
that works pretty well

00:19:36.483 --> 00:19:39.960
for getting those teams, not
only access to the data science

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:42.540
resources that they need, but
also getting those data science

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:46.110
resources more ingrained with
what the solution looks like,

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:49.573
or actually the different
pieces that they're working on.

00:19:49.573 --> 00:19:51.990
Are there some challenges to
this that other people aren't

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:53.490
picking up this same structure?

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:55.300
Or is that the same reason why?

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:55.620
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah, no.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:57.420
I think there's definitely
some challenges.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:59.490
Like one of the ones that
we deal with is that--

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:01.878
I think we have nine data
scientists now under me.

00:20:01.878 --> 00:20:03.420
You know, we're
everywhere in Devoted

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:04.380
all over the organization.

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:06.120
And it means a lot of times
there's only a single data

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:07.330
scientist sitting in a meeting.

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:08.747
So sometimes those
data scientists

00:20:08.747 --> 00:20:10.860
could actually be the lone
resource for something.

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:13.110
And that means that it can be a
situation where they don't feel

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:14.527
like they get
support, and we need

00:20:14.527 --> 00:20:16.770
to make sure that we
actively encourage that.

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:17.728
That is a real problem.

00:20:17.728 --> 00:20:19.103
I mean, the other
one, of course,

00:20:19.103 --> 00:20:21.660
is that managing a group of
people who are nine people who

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:24.300
are across like, 15 different
projects across the company,

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:26.610
means that I jump around
all over the place.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:29.160
I'm in all aspects of
what the company does,

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.515
only for like five-minute
segments before I

00:20:31.515 --> 00:20:32.880
go on to the next part.

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.070
It is like anything else
with cost and benefits.

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:37.587
I wouldn't do it another way
at a company like Devoted,

00:20:37.587 --> 00:20:39.420
but I completely see
the value if, like, you

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:41.837
were more of a product company,
like a straight technology

00:20:41.837 --> 00:20:44.610
product company, to actually
have everyone sit in one room,

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:46.980
and then bust out the Jira
board and say, OK, cool,

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:49.230
like I'm going to manage
what every single person does

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:49.890
[INAUDIBLE].

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:52.628
We're in fact like, I am
doing a lot more coordinating.

00:20:52.628 --> 00:20:54.170
I'm doing a lot
more, like, guidance.

00:20:54.170 --> 00:20:55.280
I'm doing a lot more,
like, hey, if you

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:56.400
need to pull the
emergency ejection handle,

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:57.720
like, call me in and
I'll help you out

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:58.560
with this kind of stuff.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:20:59.940
That is like a new
structure, and I

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:01.065
think we do it pretty well.

00:21:01.065 --> 00:21:03.700
But we are learning all the time
about how we can do it better.

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:06.252
We do retros, so we do, like,
retrospectives of the last two

00:21:06.252 --> 00:21:07.210
weeks, every two weeks.

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:09.502
So we're constantly, like,
sending cells like, how well

00:21:09.502 --> 00:21:10.650
are we doing this thing?

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:12.300
Because there isn't a lot
of guidebooks about that.

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:13.470
There's no standard
structure for this.

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:14.880
We're still figuring
this stuff out.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:16.800
MICHELLE CASBON: Number one,
how do you scale that approach?

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:18.150
Do you see that changing?

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:20.040
Like, Devoted's
pretty hyper growth.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.545
And having nine
people under you,

00:21:21.545 --> 00:21:23.670
like, you can still manage
that as a single person,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:25.770
but very quickly you'll
to have other people.

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:27.103
So number one, how do you scale?

00:21:27.103 --> 00:21:30.108
And then also, like, as an IC,
how do I not get overwhelmed?

00:21:30.108 --> 00:21:31.650
Like how do I actually
get work done,

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:32.820
instead of getting
sort of dragged

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:34.470
into a lot of the
business details?

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:34.770
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:37.170
I mean, the interesting thing
about being in a company that

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:38.628
is hyper growth--
and I really feel

00:21:38.628 --> 00:21:40.403
like that is very,
like, a tech bro

00:21:40.403 --> 00:21:41.653
saying or something like that.

00:21:41.653 --> 00:21:42.233
[LAUGHING]

00:21:42.233 --> 00:21:43.650
But, I mean, it's
true at Devoted.

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:45.025
Like, when I joined
Devoted there

00:21:45.025 --> 00:21:46.280
was something like 60 people.

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:49.890
And our last incoming class
of folks was 50 people.

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:51.660
So like, just massive growth.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:52.440
MICHELLE CASBON: Those
are large numbers.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:53.790
CHRIS ALBON: Those
are large numbers.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:56.248
There's a lot more people who
are joining every single day.

00:21:56.248 --> 00:21:59.950
And as we grow, there has to
be more structure in place.

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:02.448
And the thing that I really
am fighting for a lot

00:22:02.448 --> 00:22:04.740
and trying to restructure in
a way that works for that,

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:06.630
is to allow us to
cover everything,

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:09.480
but also to allow our data
scientists to sit down and work

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.280
on something for a
long period of time.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:12.790
You know, it almost feels
like zone defense, right.

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:14.520
You're like, just
covering everything.

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:16.110
Then someone comes over and
taps you on the shoulder

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:17.490
and says, hey, could
you just do this for me?

00:22:17.490 --> 00:22:19.050
And you stop what you're
doing and you help them out

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:20.130
for three hours.

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:21.630
And then someone else
taps you on the shoulder.

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:22.800
As opposed to
saying, hey, this is

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:24.675
a big, long, difficult
project that I'm going

00:22:24.675 --> 00:22:26.730
to work on for four months.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:27.527
Don't bother me.

00:22:27.527 --> 00:22:29.610
We're trying to like, split
up a team a little bit

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:30.930
so we actually folks
that are doing more,

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:32.670
like, able to be
distracted by anything.

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:34.380
You know, if anybody wants to
come and ask a question, they

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:35.970
can come and help,
while other folks are

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:37.345
sitting down and
actually working

00:22:37.345 --> 00:22:40.050
on stuff for long winds,
right, big, longer winds.

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.630
And probably build that out
more and more as time goes on,

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:44.190
because you do want to make
sure that we are covering

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:45.480
the needs for everything.

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:47.647
Also, there are some larger
wins like things like ML

00:22:47.647 --> 00:22:49.230
and that kind of
stuff that we're just

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:50.970
starting to build
into, which require

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:53.800
a lot longer process before
you actually see value from it.

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:53.870
Right.

00:22:53.870 --> 00:22:55.590
You have to build out this whole
infrastructure of how you train

00:22:55.590 --> 00:22:57.298
the models and manage
the models and then

00:22:57.298 --> 00:22:58.410
apply 'em to our system.

00:22:58.410 --> 00:22:59.280
That takes time.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:00.155
MICHELLE CASBON: Yes.

00:23:00.155 --> 00:23:02.490
I have so many questions
about that, in fact.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:04.740
But before we get on
to the infrastructure

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:06.840
that you're using and what
your stack looks like,

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.270
I am curious in
hearing more about

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:10.690
how you've grown your team.

00:23:10.690 --> 00:23:13.417
So with all of these people that
are coming in, some of those

00:23:13.417 --> 00:23:14.250
are data scientists.

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:16.500
How have you been purposeful
about who you hire,

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:17.830
who you bring onto the team?

00:23:17.830 --> 00:23:20.310
What have been your
driving factors in building

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:21.050
out your team?

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:21.800
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:24.400
So when you do the
fully integrated model,

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:27.280
one of the things that I had
a hypothesis of the start,

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:28.710
which has played
out very well, is

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:31.350
that your data scientists
need to have really

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:32.700
high emotional intelligence.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:34.573
They need to be able
to sit on other teams.

00:23:34.573 --> 00:23:36.240
Other teams need to
want them to come in

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.323
and say, hey, like, come
sit in my meeting, right.

00:23:38.323 --> 00:23:40.810
If you walk in and you're a
detriment to their meeting,

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:42.330
they're not going to invite
you to come in again, right.

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:44.040
They're not going
to want you to join

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.010
the team, like sit in
with them while they are

00:23:46.010 --> 00:23:47.120
trying to get their work done.

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:49.537
MICHELLE CASBON: I think that's
my favorite quote of like,

00:23:49.537 --> 00:23:51.593
the last year is that
your people need to have

00:23:51.593 --> 00:23:52.760
high emotional intelligence.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:54.140
You don't hear people
talking about that.

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:56.222
So you've taken a
purposeful approach to that.

00:23:56.222 --> 00:23:57.180
How have you done that?

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:58.590
How have you substantiated that?

00:23:58.590 --> 00:23:59.340
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:01.190
So it's part of our
interview process.

00:24:01.190 --> 00:24:02.700
So, like, we do a
number of things,

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:04.580
but the one that
actually works really

00:24:04.580 --> 00:24:07.493
well is that for us, anyone
who interviews someone

00:24:07.493 --> 00:24:09.410
or talks to someone, or
does any kind of thing

00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:11.480
with any candidate has
full veto right to say,

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:12.620
I don't want to
work this person.

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:14.210
Like, this person probably
wouldn't be good for me,

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:15.060
and I'll nix 'em.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:15.415
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:24:15.415 --> 00:24:17.082
CHRIS ALBON: I'll
remove 'em right away.

00:24:17.082 --> 00:24:19.190
And that offer, when
people are talking to 'em--

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:21.050
you know, when one of
my folks is actually

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:23.323
talking to a candidate,
that offer from me to say,

00:24:23.323 --> 00:24:24.240
hey, if you have any--

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:25.948
you don't even need
to like, you know, do

00:24:25.948 --> 00:24:27.600
some elaborate
explanation as to why.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:29.420
If you don't feel like you
can work with that person,

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:31.420
let's just get rid of 'em
and find someone else.

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:34.940
It is incredibly important,
because the fully integrated

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:37.580
model that we do,
doesn't work if you're

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:39.510
the brilliant [BLEEP].

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:41.260
It's not something
that works really well

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:43.490
because if you come
in and, you know,

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:45.090
someone is a very
good coder, which

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:47.090
I'm not sure the brilliant
[BLEEP] like actually

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:47.910
exists in reality.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.010
But like, if someone comes in
and they are very, very smart,

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:51.468
but no one wants
to work with them,

00:24:51.468 --> 00:24:53.510
it harms the team
internally, because people

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:54.950
don't want to ask those
stupid questions, which

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:56.408
is so critical when
you're learning

00:24:56.408 --> 00:24:57.950
a new field like
Medicare Advantage.

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:00.492
And other people at the company
don't want to work with them,

00:25:00.492 --> 00:25:01.742
thus they don't invite 'em in.

00:25:01.742 --> 00:25:03.440
They don't ask me to
like, send someone

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:05.583
on to their team to help
them out with a problem.

00:25:05.583 --> 00:25:07.250
And so are very, very
careful with that.

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:08.780
And I don't have,
obviously my team

00:25:08.780 --> 00:25:10.010
sitting in this room with
me, but if you actually

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:12.410
looked at the team you'd see
that people are very kind

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:14.100
and very honest and very open.

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:16.475
And that's something that
we've selected for, explicitly.

00:25:16.475 --> 00:25:18.558
MARK MIRCHANDANI: How do
you give advice to people

00:25:18.558 --> 00:25:20.463
to avoid that specific
problem, or to avoid

00:25:20.463 --> 00:25:21.380
becoming that problem?

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:23.255
Because I think it's a
stereotype that we all

00:25:23.255 --> 00:25:26.160
have heard of, like the rock
star developer or the rock data

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:27.890
scientist-- whatever
it is, right.

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:29.720
Like this idea that
someone knows enough

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:31.390
that they can just go off
and do their own thing,

00:25:31.390 --> 00:25:33.307
and they don't need other
people to help them.

00:25:33.307 --> 00:25:35.910
I think it's common enough
that people know about it,

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:39.055
and it can be a really difficult
trap for people to fall into.

00:25:39.055 --> 00:25:40.430
What advice do
you give to people

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:42.440
to say, like, don't
become this person?

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:43.940
CHRIS ALBON: I think
the easiest way

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:46.300
that people fall in it is
when the data science team is

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:48.800
actually isolated from the rest
of the organization-- right,

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.235
if they're a research
shop off in the corner,

00:25:51.235 --> 00:25:53.360
it is so easy to fall into
that, because you're not

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:54.340
selecting that.

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:56.090
On an everyday basis
you don't need people

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:57.650
with lots and lots of
emotional intelligence

00:25:57.650 --> 00:25:59.780
if there's just four
engineers sitting in a room.

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:01.667
I think in reality
you do, but that's

00:26:01.667 --> 00:26:03.500
the common perception,
right, that you could

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.510
find four great coders
and put 'em in the room

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:06.620
and call it a day.

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:08.543
That's not actually
the case, but when

00:26:08.543 --> 00:26:10.460
it becomes a fully
integrated model and people

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:12.758
are actually working
with non-technical folks

00:26:12.758 --> 00:26:14.300
throughout the
organization, and have

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:16.092
to respect those people's
knowledge, right.

00:26:16.092 --> 00:26:19.040
They've been working in
this space for 20 years,

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:20.820
you can't really be
condescending to them

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:21.320
in any way.

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:21.500
Right.

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:22.790
They know what they're doing.

00:26:22.790 --> 00:26:23.960
You're there to
support 'em, and you

00:26:23.960 --> 00:26:25.580
can be an equal partner
in that, and challenge

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:27.080
some of their ideas,
but you do have

00:26:27.080 --> 00:26:28.400
to respect their knowledge.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:31.940
Being technical is not the
advantage in this space.

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:35.070
And when you have that, it
becomes very, very obvious,

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:37.970
very, very quickly that not
having emotional intelligence,

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:39.980
and not being able
to work with folks

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:41.270
in that way is a detriment.

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:43.940
It's very, very quick that you
realize that that doesn't work.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:46.273
I get it that there's another
scenario where it's harder

00:26:46.273 --> 00:26:48.200
to see, right, because
some of the engineers

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:51.490
just don't work as well, because
there's some brilliant [BLEEP]

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:52.970
in the room or
something like that.

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:55.160
It's harder as the manager TO
normally see that as the case,

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:55.280
right.

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:57.270
Because the other engineers
basically close their mouth.

00:26:57.270 --> 00:26:58.070
They don't really talk about it.

00:26:58.070 --> 00:26:59.945
They just kind of like,
work in a certain way

00:26:59.945 --> 00:27:02.540
that it's not optimal, but
they don't really express it.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:05.050
When technical folks are
working with non-technical folks

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:07.110
throughout the organization
it becomes really obvious.

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:08.870
And so I get it that
people fall into this trap.

00:27:08.870 --> 00:27:10.090
It's totally reasonable.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:13.515
I think being in any kind
of technology organization,

00:27:13.515 --> 00:27:15.140
there's like the
technical side, right.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:17.510
There's the code and the
servers and the config

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:20.090
files and all that stuff, and
then there's a social machine.

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:22.790
There's this other part of
people's goals and desires

00:27:22.790 --> 00:27:24.540
and emotions and all
that kind of stuff.

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.360
And if you don't
address that, you're

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:27.740
not going to be
everywhere in the company.

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:27.890
Right.

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:30.320
You're not going to be doing
the best that you could do.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:31.267
And that part of it--

00:27:31.267 --> 00:27:33.600
If I had some like, lasting
lesson from this whole thing

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:35.850
as I go forward is that
focusing on the social machine

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:38.240
explicitly, like making sure
that all your folks can work

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.250
with everyone, is a
game-changer that's

00:27:40.250 --> 00:27:41.640
completely unappreciated.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:43.280
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, I
totally agree with that.

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:45.238
It's not something that
people talk about much.

00:27:45.238 --> 00:27:48.057
But in my experience as
well, if you can't get along,

00:27:48.057 --> 00:27:50.390
if you don't have kindness
and respect in the workplace,

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:52.880
people who would otherwise
be very brilliant,

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:55.190
just get sort of
quieted, and they're

00:27:55.190 --> 00:27:57.110
afraid to ask the
right questions.

00:27:57.110 --> 00:28:00.080
And that means that the business
case doesn't get addressed.

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:02.700
So thank you for talking about
that, for bringing that up.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.270
What are some ways that
you can support your teams?

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:07.220
If you have a group of
people in a room that

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:09.470
have respect for each other
and are kind to each other

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:11.900
and are brilliant, can
address the business problems,

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:13.250
how do you support them
in that and make sure

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:14.792
that they continue
to be comfortable,

00:28:14.792 --> 00:28:16.332
that they can continue to grow?

00:28:16.332 --> 00:28:18.290
What are some things that
you do for your team?

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:19.040
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:20.520
So two of them I
do very clearly.

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:23.030
One, I'm the first person
to ask the dumbest question.

00:28:23.030 --> 00:28:25.280
Like, I'm very, very explicit
I do not know everything

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:26.060
about Medicare Advantage.

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:26.780
I know a lot.

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:28.597
I know way more than
the average person.

00:28:28.597 --> 00:28:29.930
I work in this every single day.

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:31.640
I've spent a lot of time
learning this stuff,

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:33.110
and I'm still learning
stuff, and I'm very

00:28:33.110 --> 00:28:34.402
open to something I don't know.

00:28:34.402 --> 00:28:36.920
I'm not pretending I know every
single fact that comes in.

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:38.448
I will ask it if
I don't know it.

00:28:38.448 --> 00:28:40.240
And being the senior
person in the room who

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:42.217
is open and available
to ask that question,

00:28:42.217 --> 00:28:44.300
of which some of my team
actually know the answer,

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:46.028
and they can actually
answer to me--

00:28:46.028 --> 00:28:46.820
that is a big deal.

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:48.080
Like being the first
person that comes in--

00:28:48.080 --> 00:28:50.443
and when we did the Slack
channel that actually allowed

00:28:50.443 --> 00:28:51.860
everyone to answer
every question,

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:53.120
I was the first people
to ask the question.

00:28:53.120 --> 00:28:54.770
I'm happy to go in and say,
I don't know something.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:56.812
That your senior folks
are asking those questions

00:28:56.812 --> 00:28:57.900
is the big deal.

00:28:57.900 --> 00:29:00.170
The other one is to be
very explicit about it.

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:01.850
You don't need to be
coy about the fact

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:03.440
that you value
emotional intelligence

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:05.158
or like, some kind
of like, social game.

00:29:05.158 --> 00:29:07.700
Just say it explicitly, like,
we value emotional intelligence

00:29:07.700 --> 00:29:09.700
in this company because
you need to do your job.

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:11.660
In order to do your job
the best way possible,

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:12.570
you need to do that.

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:13.760
You need to go and
relate with people.

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:14.870
You need to be able
to work with people.

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:17.103
People need to like you
and want to work with you.

00:29:17.103 --> 00:29:18.770
That is an explicit
thing that we value,

00:29:18.770 --> 00:29:20.480
and I say it
explicitly to my folks.

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:23.063
If you don't do that, I don't
understand how that works.

00:29:23.063 --> 00:29:23.980
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah.

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:24.817
That's amazing.

00:29:24.817 --> 00:29:26.400
You're looking at
hiring a few people.

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:28.730
And I looked at your job
ad and I've never seen

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:30.560
so many instances
of the word, like,

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:32.900
"love," and "care,"
in a tech job.

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:34.420
Ad I was really blown away.

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:36.810
It's clear, not just
having this conversation,

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:38.482
but it's clear how
the company is run.

00:29:38.482 --> 00:29:40.190
Like, this is a thing
that we care about,

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:41.540
that we're explicit about it.

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:42.930
I'm glad that's working for you.

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:43.513
Keep doing it.

00:29:43.513 --> 00:29:44.180
[LAUGHING]

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:46.010
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And it sounds
like a lot of these things,

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:48.050
you know, you may have
practiced and learned

00:29:48.050 --> 00:29:49.820
before you came to
Devoted, because you

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:52.635
have quite a, kind of a
storied history, as it were.

00:29:52.635 --> 00:29:54.010
CHRIS ALBON:
[INAUDIBLE] history.

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:54.914
[LAUGHING]

00:29:55.820 --> 00:29:56.480
OK, Yeah.

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:56.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:29:56.780 --> 00:29:58.820
I mean, well, that's the
best term I can come up--

00:29:58.820 --> 00:29:59.990
I don't know if you
have a preferred term.

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:00.657
CHRIS ALBON: No.

00:30:00.657 --> 00:30:02.780
I just-- I've never
been "storied," I guess.

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:03.280
[LAUGHING]

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:05.655
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well I'd
love to hear a little bit more

00:30:05.655 --> 00:30:07.845
about how you became
a data scientist,

00:30:07.845 --> 00:30:10.220
or how you decided that this
was the right route for you.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:12.410
And you had mentioned earlier
all the different organizations

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:13.243
you had worked with.

00:30:13.243 --> 00:30:16.050
So I'm sure there's a tremendous
amount of like, cool background

00:30:16.050 --> 00:30:16.550
there.

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:17.300
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:20.872
So I did my PhD in political
science at UC Davis.

00:30:20.872 --> 00:30:22.205
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Wait a second.

00:30:22.205 --> 00:30:23.940
Poly Sci isn't data science.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:24.780
CHRIS ALBON: Uh huh.

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:27.020
See, so I dragged this in here.

00:30:27.020 --> 00:30:29.392
UC Davis is a quantitative
political science program.

00:30:29.392 --> 00:30:30.850
So I don't know--
like, honestly, I

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:32.017
have no idea who Kant is.

00:30:32.017 --> 00:30:33.725
Like I have the vaguest
idea who Kant is.

00:30:33.725 --> 00:30:36.080
Like Rousseau is like
a name that I've heard,

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:37.510
but like, everything else--

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:38.510
I did statistics, right.

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:39.593
Statistics was everything.

00:30:39.593 --> 00:30:42.230
That was applied statistics
was my whole education for six

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:42.830
years.

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:45.898
When I was doing that PhD,
I got to know some folks who

00:30:45.898 --> 00:30:46.940
were working at LinkedIn.

00:30:46.940 --> 00:30:50.040
And this includes DJ and this
includes a few other folks.

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:53.240
And they were doing
such cool applied stuff

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:55.700
that I knew I wanted to
leave academia and do it.

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:58.125
They were doing so many
interesting things, a lot of it

00:30:58.125 --> 00:30:59.750
with [INAUDIBLE] at
the time, but a lot

00:30:59.750 --> 00:31:01.640
of really interesting stuff.

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:04.768
And when I saw that I knew
that I wanted to leave academia

00:31:04.768 --> 00:31:06.060
and work on that kind of stuff.

00:31:06.060 --> 00:31:08.300
And so when I got out I
talked to a friend of mine

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:11.090
who was working at a US-UK
Kenyan nonprofit called

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:14.060
FrontlineSMS that did low
resource SMS messaging

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:16.470
for, like, election monitoring
and that kind of stuff.

00:31:16.470 --> 00:31:18.045
And I was like, I
want a job, Frank.

00:31:18.045 --> 00:31:19.560
Give me a job.

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:22.582
And that was an incredibly
humbling experience,

00:31:22.582 --> 00:31:24.540
as someone who thought
of themselves as like, I

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:25.680
have a PhD, I'm amazing.

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:27.930
And then you fly over to
Morocco and then you're like,

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:28.627
oh, like--

00:31:28.627 --> 00:31:29.960
MICHELLE CASBON: I have no idea.

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:32.180
CHRIS ALBON: I have no idea
what's happening, right.

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:35.992
And, you know, the most humbling
experience of that, by far,

00:31:35.992 --> 00:31:38.450
was that we would do trainings
with folks and teach 'em how

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:39.260
to use--

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:41.120
our software as free and
open-source, and so we'd teach

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:41.810
'em how to use it.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:43.820
And we'd give them some, like,
physical pieces of hardware

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:44.720
to help 'em do it.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:47.990
And doing those trainings,
it became very explicit

00:31:47.990 --> 00:31:51.800
over the years in my mind,
this and at Ushahidi, which

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:53.150
was the next place I worked--

00:31:53.150 --> 00:31:56.060
if the cops kind of burst down
the room, right, like the cops

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:57.790
sort of swarmed in
and do it, you know,

00:31:57.790 --> 00:31:59.720
and really arrested everyone,
I was going to get on a plane,

00:31:59.720 --> 00:31:59.870
right.

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:02.240
They were just going to put me
on a plane and fly back home

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:04.157
and that was going to
be the end of everything

00:32:04.157 --> 00:32:05.823
and it wouldn't
really be a big deal.

00:32:05.823 --> 00:32:07.490
But for the other--
like the local folks

00:32:07.490 --> 00:32:09.740
in the room, the folks who
were living in that country

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:12.410
and can't leave and were on
the radar of the government

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:14.868
for the whole time and didn't
have the protection of the US

00:32:14.868 --> 00:32:17.660
embassy, it was going to
be a very, very big deal.

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:21.265
And taking their security
concerns into account,

00:32:21.265 --> 00:32:22.890
we would use all this
training material

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:24.677
and then burn it in a bonfire.

00:32:24.677 --> 00:32:26.510
So there was like
physically nothing there--

00:32:26.510 --> 00:32:26.790
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:28.070
CHRIS ALBON: --that
someone else could do.

00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:29.980
But having that respect
for the other person.

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:31.730
Like saying like, hey,
like, I know a lot.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:32.780
I know a lot about data science.

00:32:32.780 --> 00:32:34.322
I get there's lots
of hype around it.

00:32:34.322 --> 00:32:36.800
AI is super cool, all
that kind of stuff,

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:39.710
and yet the person in the
field, the person in the room,

00:32:39.710 --> 00:32:42.230
they're putting a lot on the
line, right, to work with you.

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:45.350
And having respect for that
and understanding that,

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:47.570
and respecting their
knowledge of these things

00:32:47.570 --> 00:32:50.480
is by far the greatest lesson
that I learned, doing that

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:51.702
for a good number of years.

00:32:51.702 --> 00:32:53.660
MICHELLE CASBON: Because
it doesn't necessarily

00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:54.950
matter what the application is.

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:56.570
Being able to respect
the user experience

00:32:56.570 --> 00:32:58.100
and understand what
they're going through

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:00.058
and where they're
vulnerable, and you're really

00:33:00.058 --> 00:33:02.220
being entrusted with access
to parts of their lives

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:03.390
that could endanger them.

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:05.098
And it sounds like
every data scientist

00:33:05.098 --> 00:33:06.890
should have an experience
that really helps

00:33:06.890 --> 00:33:08.932
them connect with their
users, and really respect

00:33:08.932 --> 00:33:10.100
the access that they have.

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:10.850
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:33:10.850 --> 00:33:13.228
And I think it applies,
you know, well beyond--

00:33:13.228 --> 00:33:15.770
I mean, these are very serious
cases like election monitoring

00:33:15.770 --> 00:33:16.970
and health programs.

00:33:16.970 --> 00:33:18.590
But in my mind it
applies everywhere.

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:20.210
If you work at a food
delivery company, like,

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:22.040
you really need to understand
how people are using it.

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:23.748
Like, you know, you
might think that it's

00:33:23.748 --> 00:33:26.280
a 22-year-old who's using
it to get beer after work.

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:28.953
But, in fact, it's like
someone on maternity

00:33:28.953 --> 00:33:30.620
leave and they have
two kids and they're

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:32.210
trying to-- you know,
they're just struggling

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:33.510
and they just really,
really want it, and try

00:33:33.510 --> 00:33:35.720
to understand their
perspective and their wants.

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:38.780
I can't think of doing it
another way that would work.

00:33:38.780 --> 00:33:40.072
It seems arrogant.

00:33:40.072 --> 00:33:42.030
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well
it's kind of clear that,

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:44.570
like, especially from that
experience why you brought that

00:33:44.570 --> 00:33:45.642
into Devoted.

00:33:45.642 --> 00:33:47.850
And it sounds like the big
part of the culture there.

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:48.110
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:48.610
No.

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:51.947
And that was something that DJ
and I were very explicit about,

00:33:51.947 --> 00:33:54.030
that we need to-- don't
assume that you understand

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:54.660
our members' lives.

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:55.160
Right.

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:57.060
So, you know, Medicare
Advantage and Medicare

00:33:57.060 --> 00:33:59.540
are provided to every
single American over 65,

00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:01.920
so people who are rich and
people are poor, and so don't

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:05.020
understand what you know the
significance of a $5 copay is,

00:34:05.020 --> 00:34:05.520
right.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:06.937
That might be
nothing for someone,

00:34:06.937 --> 00:34:08.699
and it might be a
lot for someone.

00:34:08.699 --> 00:34:11.071
And so don't build models
that assume that you

00:34:11.071 --> 00:34:12.029
know the value of that.

00:34:12.029 --> 00:34:14.610
That kind of stuff happens
every single day, right,

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:16.290
making sure that we know that.

00:34:16.290 --> 00:34:18.483
And, you know, sometimes
I think we stumble a bit,

00:34:18.483 --> 00:34:19.900
and sometimes I
think we crush it.

00:34:19.900 --> 00:34:22.170
But just making sure we do
it better every single day,

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:23.639
that is what we
are selecting on.

00:34:23.639 --> 00:34:25.537
The technical stuff
we can train out.

00:34:25.537 --> 00:34:27.870
There's tons of people who
are super technical and super

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:29.219
awesome.

00:34:29.219 --> 00:34:32.340
We are trying to get people
who can come in and really

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:34.010
make sure that they
understand the user.

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:35.010
MICHELLE CASBON: Mm-hmm.

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:37.650
So what advice would
you give to someone

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:40.300
who's looking for a role
on a team like yours?

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:42.090
Someone who cares
about their users, who

00:34:42.090 --> 00:34:45.239
places more value on the
impact that they have,

00:34:45.239 --> 00:34:48.060
rather than just the
technology that they're using.

00:34:48.060 --> 00:34:48.810
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:50.310
You know, it's an
interesting thing,

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:52.679
because we get a lot of
applicants to our jobs.

00:34:52.679 --> 00:34:57.027
And a good number of them are
really trying to, probably--

00:34:57.027 --> 00:34:58.860
I mean it seems weird
saying this at Google,

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:01.560
but trying to please Google
with a very, very, very

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:02.610
technical analysis.

00:35:02.610 --> 00:35:03.110
Right.

00:35:03.110 --> 00:35:05.490
Like, we're going
straight to, like,

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:08.250
some kind of computer
vision, like, you know,

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:11.190
adversarial network kind of
example or something like that.

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:13.190
And that might totally
get people jobs

00:35:13.190 --> 00:35:14.170
in different places.

00:35:14.170 --> 00:35:15.753
It doesn't get people
jobs at Devoted.

00:35:15.753 --> 00:35:16.290
Right.

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:18.150
That's not something--
I much, much,

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:20.430
much prefer to have
people coming in

00:35:20.430 --> 00:35:23.850
and can demonstrate
really good consciousness

00:35:23.850 --> 00:35:25.600
of what the project
they're working on.

00:35:25.600 --> 00:35:28.260
So say if you're
analyzing crime data

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:30.330
in San Francisco or
something like that, just,

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:32.247
like, running the analysis
and then showing me

00:35:32.247 --> 00:35:34.040
a chart isn't really
a-- like, who cares?

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:34.270
Right.

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:34.890
Like, I can do that.

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:35.710
Everyone can do that.

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:37.230
But going the next step
and say, hey, no, I

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:37.890
implemented something.

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:38.765
Like I did something.

00:35:38.765 --> 00:35:41.580
I went out and took this data
and I gave it to some reporter,

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:42.997
and they got an
article published,

00:35:42.997 --> 00:35:45.240
or some kind of thing to
actually have some kind of--

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:46.740
I'm not even saying
you need to make

00:35:46.740 --> 00:35:48.660
some massive difference for it.

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:51.038
But everyone can
run some analysis

00:35:51.038 --> 00:35:52.830
on some kind of publicly
available data set

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:54.205
and demonstrate
some basic skill.

00:35:54.205 --> 00:35:56.940
Right now, like-- maybe
like five or six years ago

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:59.010
that was a great way
of getting a job.

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:00.120
Now everyone's doing that.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:02.080
But something where
I can actually say,

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:03.810
hey, no, this person
clearly understands

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:05.640
this difficult
phenomenon, whether social

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:06.720
or physical or whatever.

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:08.490
That's incredibly valuable
for me, because I can then

00:36:08.490 --> 00:36:09.770
take that person
and say, hey, I'm

00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:11.070
going to put you
into an environment

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:12.310
that you don't know
anything about.

00:36:12.310 --> 00:36:14.935
You don't really know about the
lives of people who are over 65

00:36:14.935 --> 00:36:16.080
and really poor in Florida.

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:17.170
I will help you learn it.

00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:18.930
But you need to be an active
participant in that learning,

00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:20.805
and to like, sit and
actually think that this

00:36:20.805 --> 00:36:22.263
is an important thing to learn.

00:36:22.263 --> 00:36:23.680
And we'll walk our
way through it.

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:25.805
MICHELLE CASBON: So it's
about having a full story,

00:36:25.805 --> 00:36:28.968
so starting out on a project
being intentional about what

00:36:28.968 --> 00:36:30.510
you're doing and
why you're doing it,

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:31.890
and then following
through with--

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:33.682
the technical piece is
only one part of it.

00:36:33.682 --> 00:36:35.700
And it's important, but
what's more important

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:37.670
is the larger frame
for that story.

00:36:37.670 --> 00:36:37.750
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:36:37.750 --> 00:36:40.510
And if you had something-- like
say, to take the crime example.

00:36:40.510 --> 00:36:42.450
If you analyze crime
data, like, if you

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:45.300
did some ground truthings, like
you went to the police station

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:46.800
and like, checked to
make sure things were--

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:48.210
like you pulled a few
records or something

00:36:48.210 --> 00:36:50.040
like that to see if it
actually matched-- something

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:51.510
where you can actually
say, no, no, I'm digging

00:36:51.510 --> 00:36:53.010
a little bit deeper into this.

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:54.610
And everything, again,
like, I'm not saying, hey,

00:36:54.610 --> 00:36:56.902
you have to do some massive
social good project for you

00:36:56.902 --> 00:36:58.770
to work at Devoted,
but I am saying

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:01.440
that the value of
running an analysis

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:03.120
is no longer straight
that you just

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:05.058
did some simple
machine learning thing,

00:37:05.058 --> 00:37:06.850
because everyone's done
that at this point.

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:08.600
Every single person
who applies to Devoted

00:37:08.600 --> 00:37:09.570
has an example of that.

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:11.280
But that part where you've
gone a little bit farther

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.630
and you've kind of done
some ground truthing

00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:14.100
or you've looked into
something or like,

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:16.100
you've really tried to
understand the situation,

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:18.210
like, that stands
out so quickly to me.

00:37:18.210 --> 00:37:18.900
MICHELLE CASBON:
So you're saying

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:20.610
that FizzBuzz and
TensorFlow is not going

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:21.810
to get you a job at Devoted.

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:23.893
CHRIS ALBON: It will not
get you a job at Devoted.

00:37:23.893 --> 00:37:27.992
Although I did like Joel Grus'
FizzBuzz using deep learning.

00:37:27.992 --> 00:37:29.700
MICHELLE CASBON: Oh,
he has so many more.

00:37:29.700 --> 00:37:30.520
Have you seen his--

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:31.020
yeah.

00:37:31.020 --> 00:37:32.312
He does it on, like, notebooks.

00:37:32.312 --> 00:37:34.140
He has so much more
where that came from.

00:37:34.140 --> 00:37:34.890
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:37:34.890 --> 00:37:35.880
I do like Joel.

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:36.750
MICHELLE CASBON:
He's pretty great.

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:37.620
CHRIS ALBON: That was the
example where I was like,

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:40.120
there's no way-- like if someone
came in using deep learning

00:37:40.120 --> 00:37:42.600
to do a very, very simple
program example like--

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:44.817
I mean it's funny, but it's
not what I'm looking for.

00:37:44.817 --> 00:37:46.150
MICHELLE CASBON: It's very Joel.

00:37:46.150 --> 00:37:46.900
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:47.400
[LAUGHING]

00:37:47.400 --> 00:37:49.983
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it's more
than about just the technology.

00:37:49.983 --> 00:37:52.240
It's really about applying
it and making, you know,

00:37:52.240 --> 00:37:54.480
and hopefully inciting
change because of it,

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.450
but also doing kind of
the active participation

00:37:57.450 --> 00:37:59.592
in what you're trying to
do the technology around.

00:37:59.592 --> 00:38:01.800
But let's talk a little bit
more about the technology

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:04.170
too, because that part
is also important, right.

00:38:04.170 --> 00:38:05.933
And that is a good
chunk of, you know,

00:38:05.933 --> 00:38:07.350
needing to understand
data science

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:08.683
and being able to execute on it.

00:38:08.683 --> 00:38:10.642
CHRIS ALBON: I mean that's
the baseline, right.

00:38:10.642 --> 00:38:12.760
If I don't have that,
you're no real discussion.

00:38:12.760 --> 00:38:13.050
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:15.360
So what does that stack
look like a Devoted?

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:17.520
And what technologies
do you find

00:38:17.520 --> 00:38:19.200
to be the most
interesting or useful?

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:19.950
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:22.645
So one of the most
interesting things at Devoted

00:38:22.645 --> 00:38:24.770
that you wouldn't realize
if you saw on the outside

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:29.700
is that we have built our own
system for running an insurance

00:38:29.700 --> 00:38:30.200
company.

00:38:30.200 --> 00:38:31.620
So we actually
have an application

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:33.060
that we call Orinoco
that actually

00:38:33.060 --> 00:38:35.160
runs the whole insurance
company, right,

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:37.710
everything from processing
claims to managing member

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:40.480
calls, all that kind of stuff.

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:42.840
And the plus side
of that is that we

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:44.190
control that whole system.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:46.080
So if one of our
members calls, we

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.120
have very easy access
to all the information

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:48.930
that we have on 'em,
because they're not

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:50.040
in 10 different systems.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:51.565
They're in, like, one system.

00:38:51.565 --> 00:38:53.940
The downside of that is that
that system is very complex.

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:56.070
MICHELLE CASBON: That
takes a lot of support.

00:38:56.070 --> 00:38:58.420
CHRIS ALBON: It's a very,
very, very complex system.

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:01.200
It's also a very new system,
because we're a young company.

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:05.340
And so, you know, there isn't 20
years of documentation sitting.

00:39:05.340 --> 00:39:07.800
Under those, like, for us
on the data science team,

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:10.163
that data comes down and
actually gets replicated down

00:39:10.163 --> 00:39:11.580
to Snowflake and
then we're mostly

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:13.410
working on the Snowflake layer.

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:14.970
That kind of stack,
you know, that's

00:39:14.970 --> 00:39:16.710
a very like, standard stack.

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:17.770
It works very well.

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:20.430
I think it's difficult for folks
to actually come in and try

00:39:20.430 --> 00:39:23.530
to work in that Orinoco
app and understand

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:24.530
the data that comes out.

00:39:24.530 --> 00:39:25.170
Because I think we
have something--

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:26.753
we have like 1,000
tables or something

00:39:26.753 --> 00:39:30.150
like that that comes out
of this piece of software.

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:33.000
And it's so powerful if you
learn that whole data model,

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:34.717
but learning that
data model's not easy.

00:39:34.717 --> 00:39:36.300
It's not obvious how
everything works.

00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:37.842
You really have to
sit down and like,

00:39:37.842 --> 00:39:39.463
look at other people's,
like, queries,

00:39:39.463 --> 00:39:41.880
and look at other people's
code and be like, oh, I get it.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:43.400
That's how that works.

00:39:43.400 --> 00:39:44.817
And that's not an
unusual problem,

00:39:44.817 --> 00:39:45.900
compared to everyone else.

00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:46.470
Right.

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:48.750
There was this great
article a few months ago

00:39:48.750 --> 00:39:50.850
that was like some
other large company.

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:52.610
And they tried to do
a basic, like a sum,

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:54.980
like the ages of all of their
members or something like that.

00:39:54.980 --> 00:39:56.160
And it turns out that
even that calculation

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:57.827
was incredibly difficult
to actually do,

00:39:57.827 --> 00:39:59.580
because the data is
all over the place,

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:02.040
and to get historic
data, and the data models

00:40:02.040 --> 00:40:04.382
change over time, and
all that kind of stuff.

00:40:04.382 --> 00:40:07.410
But yeah, at Devoted we use
Snowflake for our stack,

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:09.730
and then we use
Periscope for analytics,

00:40:09.730 --> 00:40:11.880
which is super nice
if you know SQL,

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:14.970
because you can just grind out
a lot of charts really fast.

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:16.998
And a lot of Airflow
is thrown in there,

00:40:16.998 --> 00:40:18.290
Kubernetes, that kind of stuff.

00:40:18.290 --> 00:40:21.217
A pretty typical startup
stack, I feel like.

00:40:21.217 --> 00:40:22.050
MICHELLE CASBON: OK.

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:24.000
And as you were building
that out, did you

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:26.640
spend more time on the
actual building of the stack,

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:28.620
or figuring out what to build?

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:30.060
CHRIS ALBON: We
were lucky enough

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:33.720
that we hired a lot
of senior folks.

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:34.908
So a lot of our folks--

00:40:34.908 --> 00:40:36.450
for example, like
Carla [INAUDIBLE],,

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:38.400
who's my partner in
data engineering,

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:41.070
so my peer in data engineering.

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:42.840
She built out a lot
of stuff at RunKeeper.

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:44.450
She was like really
high up at RunKeeper

00:40:44.450 --> 00:40:45.825
and built out
their whole system.

00:40:45.825 --> 00:40:48.415
So when she came in to run
data engineering at Devoted,

00:40:48.415 --> 00:40:50.040
she really knew what
she was going for.

00:40:50.040 --> 00:40:51.430
And that's the same thing
we did with data science.

00:40:51.430 --> 00:40:52.110
Right.

00:40:52.110 --> 00:40:53.830
I've sort of seen
these things before.

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:55.440
It's not my first
rodeo in these,

00:40:55.440 --> 00:40:57.872
being one of the early
data science hires.

00:40:57.872 --> 00:41:00.330
And so we kind of knew what we
wanted to build, and we we'd

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:02.970
seen these patterns before,
and we can apply 'em.

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:05.710
That part has been super useful.

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:08.430
It means that you have a lot of
folks with a lot of experience,

00:41:08.430 --> 00:41:11.740
and you can ask them how
the best way to do it.

00:41:11.740 --> 00:41:14.430
They've tried all the
wrong ways previously,

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:16.470
and they can decide
on the right way.

00:41:16.470 --> 00:41:18.810
But it is part of
the Devoted way

00:41:18.810 --> 00:41:20.873
that our folks are
very, very independent.

00:41:20.873 --> 00:41:22.290
And it comes from
that background,

00:41:22.290 --> 00:41:24.210
that we started with
a lot of senior hires.

00:41:24.210 --> 00:41:26.920
And now we're moving into more
like, mid and junior hires.

00:41:26.920 --> 00:41:29.250
But for a while there
we had something like 30

00:41:29.250 --> 00:41:32.640
or 40 data science
folks, and engineers.

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:34.695
And we had like two junior
folks, or something.

00:41:34.695 --> 00:41:35.570
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:41:35.570 --> 00:41:38.953
CHRIS ALBON: Like some
really small number of folks

00:41:38.953 --> 00:41:39.870
that were more junior.

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:42.078
And now obviously, like,
we're kind of rebalancing it

00:41:42.078 --> 00:41:44.050
because you need to grow.

00:41:44.050 --> 00:41:45.690
But when you have so
many senior folks,

00:41:45.690 --> 00:41:48.060
you can really forget to
put in guardrails for stuff,

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:50.498
because no one needs
any guardrails.

00:41:50.498 --> 00:41:52.290
Well seemingly no one
needs any guardrails.

00:41:52.290 --> 00:41:53.890
And then Chris breaks something.

00:41:53.890 --> 00:41:56.670
But that kind of approach of
having a very powerful stack,

00:41:56.670 --> 00:41:58.710
and a stack with very
little guardrails

00:41:58.710 --> 00:42:00.330
is something where you can
only have very senior folks.

00:42:00.330 --> 00:42:01.720
And then as you add
more junior folks,

00:42:01.720 --> 00:42:03.200
you need to sit down
and say, OK, cool.

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:05.000
We actually need to
document this stuff.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:06.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It works for
when you're in startup mode.

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:07.930
It works out pretty well.

00:42:07.930 --> 00:42:10.165
But then someone comes on
and they're like, oh, oops,

00:42:10.165 --> 00:42:11.040
I deleted everything.

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:12.460
And you're like, why did
you delete everything?

00:42:12.460 --> 00:42:13.960
It's not like we
didn't document it.

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:15.112
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:42:15.112 --> 00:42:17.070
MICHELLE CASBON: That
pesky hyper growth stage.

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:17.850
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:20.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So were
there any major decisions

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:22.737
you made down the road that
you ended up reversing on,

00:42:22.737 --> 00:42:24.820
or anything that you
discovered that kind of said,

00:42:24.820 --> 00:42:26.250
oh, we need to
swap something out?

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:27.930
MICHELLE CASBON: Or that
you would like to reverse?

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:29.150
Anything that you regret?

00:42:29.150 --> 00:42:31.108
CHRIS ALBON: Oh,
anything that I regret?

00:42:31.108 --> 00:42:32.400
I think we've done pretty well.

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:35.850
I think we've explored
some technologies.

00:42:35.850 --> 00:42:38.100
So, I mean, I'll give you
an example of something

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:38.910
I was going to [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:38.910 --> 00:42:40.118
It didn't turn out very well.

00:42:40.118 --> 00:42:42.130
We compile our
whole application.

00:42:42.130 --> 00:42:44.820
So the whole application that
runs Devoted runs and go.

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:46.470
And recompile that
all the time, which

00:42:46.470 --> 00:42:48.810
means that the engineers can
actually rename table names

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:50.310
and recompile, and
those table names

00:42:50.310 --> 00:42:51.730
percolate through
the whole system.

00:42:51.730 --> 00:42:53.772
The problem with something
like an analytics tool

00:42:53.772 --> 00:42:55.710
like Periscope-- it's
hard-wired, like it's

00:42:55.710 --> 00:42:57.820
hard-coded, the table names.

00:42:57.820 --> 00:43:00.657
And so we needed a
system for the engineers

00:43:00.657 --> 00:43:03.240
to be able to change the table
names and that to percolate in.

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:04.975
And right as we
had that problem,

00:43:04.975 --> 00:43:06.600
Periscope actually
changed their system

00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:09.390
where all the charts are
actually backed up on GitHub.

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:12.330
So we could actually just
like, replace all this stuff

00:43:12.330 --> 00:43:14.730
in GitHub and then load it
back in and everything worked.

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:16.140
But there was a moment there
where I was like, wait,

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:17.400
you want to change
all the table names,

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:18.640
like multiple times a day?

00:43:18.640 --> 00:43:20.990
That's going to
destroy everything.

00:43:20.990 --> 00:43:22.992
Why did I buy Periscope?

00:43:22.992 --> 00:43:25.200
And then it was like a week
later they introduced it.

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:26.726
And I was like, whew.

00:43:26.726 --> 00:43:27.700
That was lucky.

00:43:27.700 --> 00:43:29.613
But the thing is, I
mean, the lesson for me

00:43:29.613 --> 00:43:30.780
is that you just don't know.

00:43:30.780 --> 00:43:31.290
Right.

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:33.540
We've explored technologies
that just didn't work out.

00:43:33.540 --> 00:43:35.910
We haven't explored
it, like, so far.

00:43:35.910 --> 00:43:39.090
We've been very good, or
very lucky, one of those two,

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:41.900
where we haven't implemented
something and sat down

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:43.360
and said, oh, that was terrible.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:46.112
Like why did we do that?

00:43:46.112 --> 00:43:47.570
But, I mean, we're
a young company.

00:43:47.570 --> 00:43:48.740
I'm sure that'll happen.

00:43:48.740 --> 00:43:50.182
MICHELLE CASBON:
It's inevitable.

00:43:50.182 --> 00:43:51.140
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:52.390
It's a scenario that comes up.

00:43:52.390 --> 00:43:54.830
Now I know we're running
a little bit out of time.

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:56.247
MICHELLE CASBON:
Wait, wait, wait.

00:43:56.247 --> 00:43:58.840
I really want to ask about your
machine learning flashcards.

00:43:58.840 --> 00:44:00.582
OK.

00:44:00.582 --> 00:44:02.790
So that's one of my favorite
things that you've done.

00:44:02.790 --> 00:44:04.270
I know it has helped
me personally.

00:44:04.270 --> 00:44:06.020
I would even go so far
as to say that part

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:08.585
of the reason why I have this
job at Google now is because--

00:44:08.585 --> 00:44:09.090
CHRIS ALBON: Wow, that's--

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:10.020
MICHELLE CASBON: I
used your flashcards.

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:11.220
CHRIS ALBON: --that
makes me blush.

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:12.950
MICHELLE CASBON: So
like rewind to the point

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:15.242
where you see my name show
up in your purchase history,

00:44:15.242 --> 00:44:17.720
and that was when I
was interviewing here.

00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:18.440
I'm not kidding.

00:44:18.440 --> 00:44:19.790
But I really love them.

00:44:19.790 --> 00:44:22.270
And I found them extremely
useful at building out

00:44:22.270 --> 00:44:24.990
my TensorFlow model whenever
they asked me about FizzBuzz.

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:26.378
So what prompted you to do that?

00:44:26.378 --> 00:44:28.670
I know you were just doing
it for yourself for a while,

00:44:28.670 --> 00:44:30.920
but what made you decide
to share that with people?

00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:32.670
It's kind of a vulnerable
thing to-- like,

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:34.772
they're all hand-drawn
and they're beautiful,

00:44:34.772 --> 00:44:36.980
but I can see, if I had
started to do that, I may not

00:44:36.980 --> 00:44:38.450
have wanted to publicize that.

00:44:38.450 --> 00:44:40.040
What made you decide to share?

00:44:40.040 --> 00:44:43.880
CHRIS ALBON: I think that being
honest and open about stuff

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:45.750
has always been
my best strategy.

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:48.290
And you can see-- you
know, my personal home page

00:44:48.290 --> 00:44:52.972
has something like
600 little tutorials

00:44:52.972 --> 00:44:53.930
around how I did stuff.

00:44:53.930 --> 00:44:55.070
And I wrote those
when I was learning.

00:44:55.070 --> 00:44:57.680
So some of 'em are like not
the best way to do things.

00:44:57.680 --> 00:44:59.155
And people actually
go into GitHub

00:44:59.155 --> 00:45:01.780
and sit down and say, hey, this
is a better way of doing it now

00:45:01.780 --> 00:45:02.940
and that kind of stuff.

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:04.857
But I've always been
totally honest about what

00:45:04.857 --> 00:45:05.840
I know and don't know.

00:45:05.840 --> 00:45:08.630
I don't feel any kind of need to
pretend that I'm not something.

00:45:08.630 --> 00:45:09.130
Right.

00:45:09.130 --> 00:45:11.120
And I can do that from
a position of privilege,

00:45:11.120 --> 00:45:12.140
that when you're the
director of science

00:45:12.140 --> 00:45:13.557
at a hyper growth
unicorn company,

00:45:13.557 --> 00:45:15.258
you can kind of be
like, hey, I don't

00:45:15.258 --> 00:45:16.550
care if you think I'm not good.

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:17.750
Like clearly other people.

00:45:17.750 --> 00:45:18.650
MICHELLE CASBON: But
you weren't always that.

00:45:18.650 --> 00:45:19.530
CHRIS ALBON: I
wasn't always that.

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:20.030
Yeah.

00:45:20.030 --> 00:45:22.363
It's always worked out to me,
just to say I didn't know.

00:45:22.363 --> 00:45:23.930
And when I'd made
those flashcards--

00:45:23.930 --> 00:45:27.378
I mean, I made 'em because
I knew in interviews

00:45:27.378 --> 00:45:29.420
that some of the stuff
you just need to memorize.

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:31.018
Like what's a Brier score?

00:45:31.018 --> 00:45:32.060
You just have to know it.

00:45:32.060 --> 00:45:32.560
You know.

00:45:32.560 --> 00:45:35.323
And it's frankly like, if
you walk in to an interview

00:45:35.323 --> 00:45:37.490
and you can write it on the
board, just from memory,

00:45:37.490 --> 00:45:39.440
it looks freaking impressive.

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:41.120
But my thing was
just to learn it.

00:45:41.120 --> 00:45:42.950
Some of these things, you
know, you have to sit down

00:45:42.950 --> 00:45:43.760
and you just memorize it.

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:44.677
Like what is a T-test?

00:45:44.677 --> 00:45:46.700
Like what is exactly
happening with everything?

00:45:46.700 --> 00:45:48.170
You can read it in a book once.

00:45:48.170 --> 00:45:50.890
The best way is just to extract
those pieces of information

00:45:50.890 --> 00:45:53.390
and just review it over and
over again and then you know it,

00:45:53.390 --> 00:45:54.890
and you have it down,
and you can just pull it

00:45:54.890 --> 00:45:56.120
out whenever you need to.

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:58.730
I use those with all
of my job interviews.

00:45:58.730 --> 00:46:00.030
That's why I made 'em.

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:01.760
It was a very good experience.

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:03.830
And, you know, I mean,
over the years, sure,

00:46:03.830 --> 00:46:04.900
I think there's
been some people who

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:06.410
have said they've been
wrong in various ways.

00:46:06.410 --> 00:46:08.420
And I've double-checked
them and then like,

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:09.950
fixed them and
that kind of stuff.

00:46:09.950 --> 00:46:11.040
I don't mind being wrong.

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:11.858
It's OK.

00:46:11.858 --> 00:46:14.150
MICHELLE CASBON: I think
that's good advice in general,

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:15.860
that it's OK to be
wrong, and that's

00:46:15.860 --> 00:46:16.670
where the learning comes from.

00:46:16.670 --> 00:46:17.540
That's how we all grow.

00:46:17.540 --> 00:46:18.500
CHRIS ALBON: I would
be so surprised

00:46:18.500 --> 00:46:20.330
if these days that people
actually sat down and thought

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:21.997
someone who didn't
ask questions clearly

00:46:21.997 --> 00:46:23.750
knew everything
that was happening.

00:46:23.750 --> 00:46:24.540
Everyone--

00:46:24.540 --> 00:46:24.995
MICHELLE CASBON: Good point.

00:46:24.995 --> 00:46:26.480
CHRIS ALBON: --you know,
we all know at this point

00:46:26.480 --> 00:46:28.550
that everyone doesn't know
everything, particularly

00:46:28.550 --> 00:46:30.258
data science where
people come in from so

00:46:30.258 --> 00:46:31.610
many different perspectives.

00:46:31.610 --> 00:46:34.920
I've had some folks who knew
tons about deep learning,

00:46:34.920 --> 00:46:35.960
and they were awesome.

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:37.710
And then they didn't
know basic statistics

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:39.810
because that's just not
part of the training.

00:46:39.810 --> 00:46:40.850
It didn't come up.

00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:41.840
And it was embarrassing to them.

00:46:41.840 --> 00:46:42.150
Right.

00:46:42.150 --> 00:46:43.400
It was like, what's a T-test?

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:45.870
I don't know, but I can do
this incredibly complicated

00:46:45.870 --> 00:46:47.334
adversarial network thing.

00:46:47.334 --> 00:46:49.542
MICHELLE CASBON: I call
these libraries and it works.

00:46:49.542 --> 00:46:50.810
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:46:50.810 --> 00:46:52.613
And, you know, or
people who come

00:46:52.613 --> 00:46:55.030
in from statistics and they're
like, oh, what's a feature?

00:46:55.030 --> 00:46:57.262
And I'll be like,
that's a column.

00:46:57.262 --> 00:46:59.720
That kind of stuff, it's just
always worked out to me, just

00:46:59.720 --> 00:47:00.913
to be honest and just open.

00:47:00.913 --> 00:47:03.080
You know, I started out
because it was useful for me

00:47:03.080 --> 00:47:04.430
to grow and learn.

00:47:04.430 --> 00:47:07.330
And at this point it's just,
I guess, a personal philosophy

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:08.572
that I just do.

00:47:08.572 --> 00:47:10.280
MICHELLE CASBON: Can
you share, maybe one

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:11.738
of your favorite
stories of someone

00:47:11.738 --> 00:47:13.280
who's used them and
shared with you,

00:47:13.280 --> 00:47:14.863
like, this is how
it impacted my life?

00:47:14.863 --> 00:47:16.120
Thank you for providing this.

00:47:16.120 --> 00:47:17.240
What's your favorite story

00:47:17.240 --> 00:47:17.990
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:47:17.990 --> 00:47:19.490
So there's been a
few cases of this.

00:47:19.490 --> 00:47:20.930
So I sell the cards.

00:47:20.930 --> 00:47:24.140
But if anybody can't afford
them, they can just ask me

00:47:24.140 --> 00:47:24.890
and I'll give 'em.

00:47:24.890 --> 00:47:26.150
Because it's a digital product.

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:27.620
And I've given out thousands
and thousands of cards

00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:28.203
at this point.

00:47:28.203 --> 00:47:30.720
And every so often, maybe
like once every six months,

00:47:30.720 --> 00:47:33.260
someone has come back
to me and said, hey,

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:35.240
you gave me the cards for free.

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:36.920
The cards made me get a job.

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:38.210
I have now bought the cards.

00:47:38.210 --> 00:47:39.320
Like that right there.

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:40.120
That's impact.

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:40.620
Right.

00:47:40.620 --> 00:47:42.170
Like someone who
couldn't afford it

00:47:42.170 --> 00:47:44.503
before because they were
student and they were too poor,

00:47:44.503 --> 00:47:45.673
I gave it to them for free.

00:47:45.673 --> 00:47:47.090
They ended up
studying it, getting

00:47:47.090 --> 00:47:49.170
a job in machine learning, and
then coming back and saying,

00:47:49.170 --> 00:47:51.860
hey, like, I was able to
accomplish this because of you.

00:47:51.860 --> 00:47:52.960
Let me buy a card
for someone else.

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:54.460
Like let me move
this thing forward.

00:47:54.460 --> 00:47:55.440
That is awesome.

00:47:55.440 --> 00:47:57.320
There's no other way to say you
have an impact other than that.

00:47:57.320 --> 00:47:58.778
MICHELLE CASBON:
That's a great way

00:47:58.778 --> 00:48:00.110
to scale your personal impact.

00:48:00.110 --> 00:48:01.020
I like that.

00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:01.520
OK.

00:48:01.520 --> 00:48:03.240
So I think we're
running low on time.

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:05.690
Is there any last thing
you'd like to share with us?

00:48:05.690 --> 00:48:07.370
Maybe anything you'd
like to plug or--

00:48:07.370 --> 00:48:08.120
CHRIS ALBON: Sure.

00:48:08.120 --> 00:48:11.270
We at Devoted are hiring
two folks onto my team,

00:48:11.270 --> 00:48:13.510
focusing on analytics.

00:48:13.510 --> 00:48:15.785
You know, if you Google
Devoted Health jobs,

00:48:15.785 --> 00:48:17.160
I'm pretty sure
the job comes up.

00:48:17.160 --> 00:48:18.350
You can go to my Twitter
to actually learn,

00:48:18.350 --> 00:48:20.210
where I actually talk about the
job and I'm really open about

00:48:20.210 --> 00:48:21.450
what it is and what it isn't.

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:23.200
It would be a perfect
job for a few folks.

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:24.870
MICHELLE CASBON: And we'll add
that link to the show notes.

00:48:24.870 --> 00:48:25.870
And again, I've seen it.

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:27.748
There's a lot of
love and care in it.

00:48:27.748 --> 00:48:28.790
It's a great description.

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:30.860
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And some great
flashcards to help you prepare.

00:48:30.860 --> 00:48:31.610
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah.

00:48:31.610 --> 00:48:32.870
Exactly right.

00:48:32.870 --> 00:48:33.030
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah.

00:48:33.030 --> 00:48:34.670
We'll have that link in
the show notes as well.

00:48:34.670 --> 00:48:36.260
But I can't imagine
anything better

00:48:36.260 --> 00:48:39.070
to prepare for an interview with
Chris than his own flashcards.

00:48:39.070 --> 00:48:40.320
CHRIS ALBON: Flashcards, yeah.

00:48:40.320 --> 00:48:40.550
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:48:40.550 --> 00:48:41.690
Does someone ever
like, bring in,

00:48:41.690 --> 00:48:43.520
like a printed out
version of the flashcards

00:48:43.520 --> 00:48:44.340
to your own interview?

00:48:44.340 --> 00:48:46.210
MICHELLE CASBON: And
ask you to autograph it?

00:48:46.210 --> 00:48:46.990
CHRIS ALBON: I've seen printed--

00:48:46.990 --> 00:48:48.985
I've never seen a
printed one in person.

00:48:48.985 --> 00:48:50.860
But a lot of people do
print 'em out and then

00:48:50.860 --> 00:48:51.970
send me photos of 'em.

00:48:51.970 --> 00:48:54.220
This one institute actually
pasted them on the wall

00:48:54.220 --> 00:48:55.748
like wallpaper.

00:48:55.748 --> 00:48:57.290
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
That's pretty cool.

00:48:57.290 --> 00:48:59.200
CHRIS ALBON: Yeah,
it was pretty sweet.

00:48:59.200 --> 00:49:00.250
MICHELLE CASBON: Why
didn't I print them out

00:49:00.250 --> 00:49:01.167
to get your autograph.

00:49:01.167 --> 00:49:02.513
What was I thinking?

00:49:02.513 --> 00:49:04.180
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We
can fix that later.

00:49:04.180 --> 00:49:06.105
MICHELLE CASBON:
I'll run upstairs.

00:49:06.105 --> 00:49:08.480
All right, Chris, thank you
so much for joining us today.

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:09.370
It was great to have you here.

00:49:09.370 --> 00:49:10.520
CHRIS ALBON: It's
great to be on.

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:12.978
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thank you so
much to Chris for coming in.

00:49:12.978 --> 00:49:15.782
I mean, it was a super cool
chance for us to chat with him.

00:49:15.782 --> 00:49:17.740
You know, he's got some
very interesting things

00:49:17.740 --> 00:49:19.090
that I would never
have thought about,

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:21.215
when talking-- especially
about hiring and managing

00:49:21.215 --> 00:49:21.760
data science.

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:21.970
Right.

00:49:21.970 --> 00:49:23.500
Because, you know, when
I think of data science,

00:49:23.500 --> 00:49:25.960
I think of a very technical,
very heavy skill set,

00:49:25.960 --> 00:49:28.210
in terms of needing to have
a lot of background there.

00:49:28.210 --> 00:49:31.450
But there's so much more empathy
and understanding in how to,

00:49:31.450 --> 00:49:33.430
like, kind of work
within a team and really

00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:36.340
understand why you are
doing data science,

00:49:36.340 --> 00:49:38.710
that it seems like he takes
in as a big factor to being

00:49:38.710 --> 00:49:39.610
successful.

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:40.860
MICHELLE CASBON: That's right.

00:49:40.860 --> 00:49:43.240
And in many ways it's
fairly straightforward

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:46.480
to evaluate a piece
of code or something

00:49:46.480 --> 00:49:48.545
that's very tangible
and very well defined.

00:49:48.545 --> 00:49:50.170
But emotional
intelligence is something

00:49:50.170 --> 00:49:52.240
that's a lot harder
to define, especially

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:55.090
in the limited amount of
time you have for interviews.

00:49:55.090 --> 00:49:57.807
To hear how he tries
to figure that out

00:49:57.807 --> 00:49:59.890
in advance before bringing
someone on to the team,

00:49:59.890 --> 00:50:01.750
I think that in and
of itself is a skill,

00:50:01.750 --> 00:50:05.182
because it's such a difficult
predictor to tease out.

00:50:05.182 --> 00:50:06.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So go tweet Chris

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:08.740
if you want a
reunion of "Partially

00:50:08.740 --> 00:50:10.968
Derivative," because it
sounds like that will be--

00:50:10.968 --> 00:50:11.510
I don't know.

00:50:11.510 --> 00:50:13.135
There's some serious
[INAUDIBLE] there.

00:50:13.135 --> 00:50:15.670
He sounds like he could be
convinced into doing some more.

00:50:15.670 --> 00:50:16.920
MICHELLE CASBON: That's right.

00:50:16.920 --> 00:50:19.520
I've already seen a lot of
people asking for reunions,

00:50:19.520 --> 00:50:21.970
so not just Chris but
[? Vidia ?] and Jonathan

00:50:21.970 --> 00:50:22.710
as well.

00:50:22.710 --> 00:50:24.710
It sounds like there's a
lot of demand for that.

00:50:24.710 --> 00:50:26.110
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So,
yeah, go express that,

00:50:26.110 --> 00:50:28.270
and maybe we'll all get
to come together and have

00:50:28.270 --> 00:50:29.673
a really cool reunion podcast.

00:50:29.673 --> 00:50:30.590
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah.

00:50:30.590 --> 00:50:31.300
That would be great.

00:50:31.300 --> 00:50:31.720
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Absolutely.

00:50:31.720 --> 00:50:33.510
So it was a great
conversation with Chris.

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:35.510
But I do have kind of a
follow up question here,

00:50:35.510 --> 00:50:37.570
