00:50:37.570 --> 00:50:40.444
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:50:42.840 --> 00:50:47.090
So what does it mean to
learn in machine learning?

00:50:47.090 --> 00:50:48.660
Because machines can't--

00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:51.120
I don't think they can--
they can't study like we do.

00:50:51.120 --> 00:50:52.830
They don't cram before a test.

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:55.800
They don't stay up till
4:00 AM, not that I ever

00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:57.430
did anything like that.

00:50:57.430 --> 00:51:00.030
But that learning part
of it is not as easy.

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:01.800
And people talk about
all these networks.

00:51:01.800 --> 00:51:02.710
So what does it mean?

00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:03.330
What does it mean?

00:51:03.330 --> 00:51:05.455
MICHELLE CASBON: So we're
going back to the basics.

00:51:05.455 --> 00:51:06.000
Right.

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:09.373
This may seem very
obvious, especially

00:51:09.373 --> 00:51:11.040
if you've been in the
field for a while,

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:14.770
but expressing it in words
is surprisingly difficult.

00:51:14.770 --> 00:51:17.100
This is a definition
that I got from Chris.

00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:19.188
This is one of his flashcards.

00:51:19.188 --> 00:51:20.730
This is something
that I came across.

00:51:20.730 --> 00:51:22.470
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What's the
picture on the flashcard?

00:51:22.470 --> 00:51:23.560
MICHELLE CASBON: The picture--

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:25.477
there is no picture on
this one, but the words

00:51:25.477 --> 00:51:28.050
are decorated very nicely.

00:51:28.050 --> 00:51:30.780
He includes a quote
from Tom Mitchell,

00:51:30.780 --> 00:51:33.645
who said this back in 1997.

00:51:33.645 --> 00:51:34.770
I'm going to paraphrase it.

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:37.770
If you want the full definition,
you can look at the card.

00:51:37.770 --> 00:51:41.340
But this is a great definition
for what the learn means

00:51:41.340 --> 00:51:42.300
in machine learning.

00:51:42.300 --> 00:51:44.520
To paraphrase Tom Mitchell,
a computer program

00:51:44.520 --> 00:51:48.150
is set to learn if it's
performance at specific tasks

00:51:48.150 --> 00:51:50.350
improves with experience.

00:51:50.350 --> 00:51:52.710
Well that's not all that
different from the way

00:51:52.710 --> 00:51:53.940
we learn.

00:51:53.940 --> 00:51:56.250
We read books, we
gain experience.

00:51:56.250 --> 00:51:58.050
We do tasks and we
improve at them.

00:51:58.050 --> 00:52:00.030
And with computers,
machine learning,

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:03.270
you're defining a very
narrow set of tasks.

00:52:03.270 --> 00:52:06.480
You're defining that in a way
that gives you a performance

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:06.990
indicator.

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:10.800
So you have a task, a
concrete outcome, and then

00:52:10.800 --> 00:52:14.160
you give that computer
access to experience.

00:52:14.160 --> 00:52:18.570
So you give it examples of that
task with success predictors

00:52:18.570 --> 00:52:19.980
and failure predictors.

00:52:19.980 --> 00:52:21.570
And it can take
that information,

00:52:21.570 --> 00:52:25.260
ingest it, do something
with it, and then get better

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:26.550
at those tasks.

00:52:26.550 --> 00:52:28.800
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if it
has a way to, more or less,

00:52:28.800 --> 00:52:30.540
I mean, like you
said, with experience,

00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:33.600
kind of improve or change
its algorithm to figure out

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:35.220
what it's doing
differently, that

00:52:35.220 --> 00:52:36.970
would be considered
machine learning.

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:38.760
But if someone were
to come in and modify

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:41.327
the program manually, that's
not considered machine learning.

00:52:41.327 --> 00:52:42.660
MICHELLE CASBON: That's correct.

00:52:42.660 --> 00:52:45.990
So without changing the
fundamental algorithm-- so

00:52:45.990 --> 00:52:48.663
you can change parameters
and hyper parameters.

00:52:48.663 --> 00:52:50.580
By the way, if you want
to know the difference

00:52:50.580 --> 00:52:52.520
between parameters
and hyper parameters,

00:52:52.520 --> 00:52:53.973
Chris has a flashcard for that.

00:52:53.973 --> 00:52:55.140
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Of course.

00:52:55.140 --> 00:52:55.800
MICHELLE CASBON: But
as long as you're

00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:58.217
just changing parameters and
not the fundamental algorithm

00:52:58.217 --> 00:52:59.250
for the program--

00:52:59.250 --> 00:53:03.300
so the program has the ability
to change those parameters.

00:53:03.300 --> 00:53:06.660
And it does that based
on getting access

00:53:06.660 --> 00:53:08.550
to more experience.

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:12.150
So through experience, if it
can update itself in order

00:53:12.150 --> 00:53:15.120
to get better at these tasks
so that its performance

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:17.010
indicators get better
and better and better,

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:18.300
that's machine learning.

00:53:18.300 --> 00:53:20.342
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I have
to buy these flashcards.

00:53:20.342 --> 00:53:22.430
Because if all of
that is on one card--

00:53:22.430 --> 00:53:23.640
and there's a lot of cards.

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:24.700
There's no shortage of them.

00:53:24.700 --> 00:53:24.990
Right?

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:25.830
MICHELLE CASBON: They
are hundreds of cards.

00:53:25.830 --> 00:53:26.220
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:53:26.220 --> 00:53:27.900
I mean, this is stuff
that I really just

00:53:27.900 --> 00:53:29.607
don't have a good background in.

00:53:29.607 --> 00:53:30.940
So it's super cool to hear that.

00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:32.400
And I think I like
that definition.

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:32.550
Right.

00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:33.430
It's very simple.

00:53:33.430 --> 00:53:33.930
Right.

00:53:33.930 --> 00:53:37.743
Like, if it can learn from its
experience to make changes,

00:53:37.743 --> 00:53:39.660
and usually in a positive
way, hopefully, it's

00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:41.260
the learning part
of machine learning.

00:53:41.260 --> 00:53:42.310
MICHELLE CASBON:
It's straightforward.

00:53:42.310 --> 00:53:44.910
So if you want to find out
more, including the definition

00:53:44.910 --> 00:53:49.020
